     "en": "A short story"
    },
    "text": {
     "en": "Anu read the number 12321 forwards and backwards and found it was the same.\n\"That is a palindrome,\" said her teacher."
    }
   },
   {
    "id": "explain",
    "type": "explanation",
    "provenance": "original",
    "keepEnglishTerms": [
     "palindrome",
     "reverse",
     "supercell"
    ],
    "text": {
     "en": "A palindrome reads the same from both ends, like 121 or 4554. If we reverse a number and add it to itself repeatedly, many numbers become palindromes.\nIn a supercell puzzle, a number is bigger than its neighbours in a grid. Patterns of numbers on the number line help us spot such cells.\nClocks and calendars give numbers too: 12:21 and 11:11 are palindromic times."
    }
   },
